    kind: work_item  # 7a8a
    title: "DECISION: 33 third-party rows still ship in go.yaml (unix 27, grpc functions 3, execabs 3) — reachable, unlike the 20 INV-safig moved, so culling deletes 13 measured chains. ADR-0016 §27 compliance vs default detection"  # 7a8a
    status: needs_human_review  # 7a8a
    priority: 3  # 7a8a
    parent:  # 7a8a
    tags:  # 7a8a
    - go  # 7a8a
    - io_primitives  # 7a8a
    - catalogue  # 7a8a
    - adr_0016  # 7a8a
    - needs_decision  # 7a8a
    isbefore: []  # 7a8a
    duplicate_of: []  # 7a8a
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 7a8a
    pr_ref:  # 7a8a
    description: "DECISION REQUESTED. Filed 2026-08-11 alongside INV-safig, which moved the 20\nthird-party METHOD rows out of go.yaml. These 33 are the remainder, and they are\nNOT the same case — culling them would delete working detections rather than dead\nrows, so the call is the human's.\n\nTHE REMAINDER, and why it was left:\n\n    golang.org/x/sys/unix        27 function rows\n    google.golang.org/grpc        3 function rows (Dial, DialContext, NewClient)\n    golang.org/x/sys/execabs      3 function rows\n\nAll 33 are third-party by ADR-0016's rule — `golang.org/x/...` is part of the Go\nproject but ships as a separate module, not stdlib — so on the letter of §27\n(\"a curated list of stdlib functions\") and §35 (\"a project-local overlay, not\nmore built-in rows\") they do not belong in the shipped catalogue.\n\nBUT UNLIKE THE 20, THEY WORK. `_module_matches` compares trailing components, so\n`unix` IS the trailing component of `golang.org/x/sys/unix` and the row resolves\nfrom the real import path. The 20 method rows failed only because a TYPE\nqualifier (`gin.Context`) is the trailing component of no import path. The\nthird-party function surface therefore works by accident of the suffix rule while\nthe third-party method surface could not work at all.\n\nPRICED, so the decision is not made on principle alone. Chains produced by these\n33 rows on the two Go repos measured for INV-safig's non-destruction check:\n\n    keda        4   (grpc.NewClient x4)\n    swarmkit    9   (grpc.Dial x7, grpc.DialContext x2)\n    TOTAL      13\n\n*** THE COST IS CONCENTRATED IN 3 ROWS, NOT 33. *** Every one of the 13 comes\nfrom the three grpc FUNCTION rows. The 27 `unix` rows and 3 `execabs` rows\nproduced ZERO on this cohort — though that is a 2-repo sample of higher-level\nservices, and raw `unix.*` syscalls are exactly what would appear in\nlower-level Go (container runtimes, filesystems) that this cohort does not\nrepresent. Do not read that zero as \"the unix rows are dead\"; read it as \"not\nexercised by these two repos\".\n\nTHE OPTIONS, with what each costs:\n  (a) MOVE ALL 33 to docs/io-primitives-overlays/. Fully ADR-compliant. Default\n      detection drops by 13 chains on this cohort unless the user loads the\n      overlay. The overlay already exists and already carries\n      google.golang.org/grpc.Server.Serve, so the grpc rows have an obvious home\n      beside their sibling.\n  (b) MOVE ONLY THE 3 grpc FUNCTION ROWS, beside the grpc method row already in\n      the overlay, and leave unix/execabs. Coherent for grpc, leaves 30 rows\n      still violating §27.\n  (c) KEEP ALL 33 and amend ADR-0016 to carve out `golang.org/x/...` as\n      \"effectively stdlib\". Defensible — it is maintained by the Go team under\n      the same compatibility promise — but it is an ADR amendment, not a\n      catalogue edit, and it needs the KEEP verdict's re-evaluation trigger\n      written down rather than left implicit.\n  (d) DO NOTHING and leave the inconsistency documented. Cheapest; leaves a\n      shipped catalogue that is \"stdlib-scoped\" in its docstring and not in fact.\n\nNOT MEASURED: the unix/execabs chain count on a low-level Go corpus, which is\nwhat would decide whether (a)'s cost is really 13 or substantially more. That\nmeasurement is cheap if the decision turns on it — say so and I will run it\nbefore anything is culled.\n\nI have deliberately NOT picked one. Deleting working detections is the expensive\ndirection under this project's own rule, and the ADR-compliance argument is\nstrong enough that it should be ratified rather than assumed."  # 7a8a
    fields:  # 7a8a
      statement: "The shipped io_primitives catalogue is stdlib-scoped (ADR-0016 §27); go.yaml is not, and the remaining violators are reachable rather than dead."  # 7a8a
- op: discuss  # e998
  at: '2026-08-12T03:31:02Z'  # e998
  by: agent  # e998
  actor: jgstern_agent  # e998
  clock: 2  # e998
  nonce: e998  # e998
  message: "ANSWER: OPTION (a) — MOVE ALL 33 TO THE OVERLAY. Investigated 2026-08-11 against\nthe ADRs, the spec and the record. A synthesis pass first recommended a composite\n\"respell the 30 x/sys rows and keep them built-in under a bounded ADR amendment\";\nTHREE INDEPENDENT ADVERSARIAL PASSES REFUTED IT, 3 of 3, two of them with live\nend-to-end measurements. Recording the refutation because it is the valuable part.\n\n*** THE KILL SHOT: THE RESPELL MANUFACTURES A FALSE `confirmed`. ***\n`_uncatalogued_external_modules` (verify_claims.py:781-784) compares modules by\nEXACT string equality, not by the tagger's component-aware `_module_matches`.\nToday `p.module == \"unix\"` never equals a dst module of\n`golang.org/x/sys/unix`, so the module counts as UNCATALOGUED and forces\n`inconclusive`. Respelling to the full path makes it equal — and the gate stops\nfiring. Measured, fixture whose writes go ONLY through unix.Chmod / Chown /\nFchmod / Fchown / Ftruncate / Truncate / Rename, claim \"never writes to the host\nfilesystem\":\n\n    BASE (shipped)                     exit 2  inconclusive\n    ARM R (round-trip, no edits)       exit 2  inconclusive   <- control\n    ARM C (respell + keep built-in)    exit 0  CONFIRMED      <- FALSE\n    ARM A (move all 33 to overlay)     exit 2  inconclusive   <- preserved\n    ARM C + the writes catalogued      violated, 2 chains     <- control: detectable\n\nSo the short spelling is ACCIDENTALLY PROTECTIVE, and on the gate axis\nrespell-and-keep is worse than doing nothing.\n\nWHY THE HOLE IS REAL AND NOT THEORETICAL. The `unix` row is a SUBSET of the\nstdlib `syscall` row, not a mirror of it: ten names go.yaml itself declares\nI/O-relevant under `syscall` have NO unix counterpart — Chmod, Chown, Exec,\nFchmod, Fchown, ForkExec, Ftruncate, Getdents, Rename, Truncate. Against a real\nvendored x/sys/unix (586 exported funcs): 27 catalogued = 4.6%, or 16% of its 168\nI/O-family functions; 37 of 45 write-side fs functions uncatalogued (Writev,\nSendfile, Splice, Mount, Setxattr...); 43 of 43 exec/proc uncatalogued INCLUDING\n`Exec`. Post-respell a repo spawning via `unix.Exec` could confirm \"no subprocess\nboundary\" — in the boundary that carries the completeness ratchet.\n\nTHE ADR READING WAS BACKWARDS. The synthesis rested on \"§31 retracted the\nargument beneath §27, so only §35's ownership-burden test survives\". §31 says, in\nthe amendment itself: \"The stdlib-only catalog scope is sound and stays; the\ntransitivity argument offered in support of it is not.\" That is a REAFFIRMATION\nof the conclusion in the same sentence that retracts the argument. And the\nprovenance rule is not only in Context — the DECISION section says it too:\n:115 \"a catalog of stdlib/runtime functions\", :170 naming Go's scope as\n(os, net, io, exec), :172 \"finite and stable\".\n\nTHE PRECEDENT IS ALREADY ENFORCED, AND IT IS STRONGER THAN THIS CASE. tokio::fs /\ntokio::net were 22 of 24 rows name-for-name mirrored by still-shipping std::fs /\nstd::net rows — 91.7%, HIGHER than unix's 85.2% — and were culled anyway, with\nthe rationale now machine-enforced at test_io_boundary.py:298-308: \"tokio is\nubiquitous but third-party — strict rule applies; same governance as every other\nwrapper.\" The \"everyone uses this spelling\" argument was heard and rejected once\nalready. Commit 864f55ed02 (Plan C): \"The catalog principle 'stdlib only' is now\nenforced as a strict rule\" — six languages culled, Go omitted.\n\nAND THE OWNER RULING ALREADY DECIDES IT. Recorded on INV-safig the day before\nthis item was filed, and carried in the session handoff the whole time:\n\"io_primitives stays STDLIB-SCOPED (ADR-0016 §27); third-party is USER-SUPPLIED\nvia overlays.\" That is a PROVENANCE rule, not a burden rule. I filed this item as\nan open decision when the standing ruling answered it; that was my error, and the\ninvestigation's value turned out to be the fail-open, not the choice.\n\n*** CORRECTIONS TO THIS ITEM'S OWN FILED NUMBERS ***\n- \"THE COST IS CONCENTRATED IN 3 ROWS, NOT 33\" is FALSE. On an 11-repo cohort\n  enriched for low-level Go: 111 chains, of which unix 85 (77%), grpc 24,\n  execabs 2.\n- The 13-chain figure was not a small sample, it was a STRUCTURALLY BLIND one:\n  keda and swarmkit have ZERO non-vendor `golang.org/x/sys/unix` importers, so\n  that cohort could not have shown the unix cost at any sample size. Treat 13 as\n  a floor and 111 as a worst-case ceiling; neither is an expected value, and the\n  111 was not independently re-run.\n- TAINT was never priced here and is a real cost of (a): the 33 rows seed 17 of\n  Go's 119 auto-derived sinks and 7 sources (effective default sources 41 -> 34,\n  not 38 -> 31 — 38 is the auto-derived layer only). All flag-restorable.\n\nTHE ONE GENUINE COST OF (a), which is not the chain count:\n`golang.org/x/sys/execabs.Command` / `.CommandContext` are hardcoded in\nHIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES (io_boundary.py:264) and `.LookPath` in\nHIGH_RISK_EXEMPTIONS_SUBPROCESS (:324), and the WI-sugav drift guard requires\nevery HIGH_RISK entry to resolve in a BUILT-IN catalogue. So (a) forces a\nfrozenset edit in the same PR and loses a live `high_risk=True` from default runs.\nexecabs is also the one row-set with a defensible keep-argument on its own terms\n(3 rows = ~its entire public API, already full-path spelled, already gate-covered)\n— but keeping it is exactly the per-row rule-switching that made the composite\nrecommendation incoherent, so it goes with the rest.\n\nWHY NOT (b) OR (d):\n (b) move only grpc — sheds 24 of the 111 and leaves 30 rows violating; and the\n     flag-invisibility objection used against (a) applies to it identically.\n (d) do nothing — survives on the GATE axis (the short spelling keeps repos\n     honestly `inconclusive`) and is the only branch available if the owner\n     overrides, but it leaves a live FP surface on the 4 bare sole-holders\n     (Fstatat, Mkdirat, Renameat, Unlinkat) and cannot be disclosed: there is no\n     `status:` value for over-inclusion (io_boundary.py:190-193 accepts only\n     `complete` / `in_progress`), no warning for it, and no output field for it.\n\nIF THE OWNER OVERRIDES AND KEEPS THE ROWS: keep them spelled `unix`. DO NOT\nRESPELL. The short spelling is the only thing keeping the coverage gate honest."  # e998
